choice("Continue", Ch7-God-of-death, v_current_scene = Ch7-God-of-death)


ID: Ch7-God-of-death
TEXT:

The necromancer pulls himself out of his ally's grasp and starts casting a spell.

"Tell me, Azarius," he says. "Have you ever heard of the still winter?"

"I've heard several accounts of it when I visited this continent in the past, yes," the sage answers calmly. "Supposedly, it was a great war waged on this continent between stillwaters and ancients some six hundred years ago."

"Indeed," Zack says. "What would you do if I told you that the remains of the stillwaters who died in that war can still be found deep underground, scattered throughout the continent?"

Dozens of animated skeletons claw their way out of the earth below us.

"What would you do if I told you that I could bring these stillwaters back to life as my puppets, with almost all of their former magical prowess intact?"

The skeletons begin attacking all of the mages around us as they run away, screaming in terror. The sky darkens as it starts raining fire and brimstone. Kate struggles to keep an icy roof above our heads to shield us from the flames, the heat melting it almost as fast as she reinforces it.

"What would you do, sage? Tell me!"

"Well, since you asked so politely," Azarius says, smiling. "What I would do is this."

The Sage of the West smashes his staff into the ground, and an intense vibration sweeps through the air. The skeletons freeze mid-stride, vibrating violently. Within seconds, all of their bones shatter into powder simultaneously, the rain of fire halts, and the sky clears up once more.

Zack the necromancer stands dumbstruck, looking around in disbelief at the mounds of dust.

choice("Continue", Ch7-Illusion, v_current_scene = Ch7-Illusion)


ID: Ch7-Illusion
TEXT:

"That was an impressive spell, no doubt," Azarius says. "But in the end, those skeletons were only old dried-up bones bound with magic. A powerful sonic attack shatters any object once you match its resonant frequency. Now, that spell has a duration of five minutes and can only be cast once per day. Unless you have another trick, I believe several gentlemen would like a word with you..."

All of the mages who were chased by skeletons moments ago surround the necromancer and his ally, baring their weapons.

"I'm tired of your crap, Zack," says the necromancer's companion. "Every single time. Next time we're in a town, you're buying the drinks."

The mage with the circlet throws a sphere onto the ground, which explodes into a thick gray smoke screen.

"Quick!" says one of the mages. "Disperse the smoke! Don't let them get away."

An air elementalist blows the smoke away, revealing Zack and his friend a few hundred feet out, running towards the trees.

"After them!" a mage shouts, and the crowd surges into the forest in pursuit.

Within seconds, the mages disappear into the timber, leaving only me, Daren, Kate, Hadrik, Azarius, and a teenage apprentice holding the sage's spare scrolls.

"It never ceases to amaze me how easily people are fooled by simple illusions!" Azarius remarks, stroking his white beard.

choice(""Illusion? Don't you mean smoke bomb?"", Ch7-Smoke, v_current_scene = Ch7-Smoke)
choice(""Wait, are you saying that those mages are still hiding around here, invisibly?"", Ch7-Invisibility, v_current_scene = Ch7-Invisibility)


ID: Ch7-Smoke
TEXT:

"Illusion? Don't you mean smoke bomb?"

"No, no, I was talking about what happened after the smoke. The two mages you saw run towards the forest were in fact decoys. The real mages waited invisibly for everyone to leave and then teleported away. I figured they learned their lesson, so I let them off with a stern warning for now."

"Barry! Kate! Daren!" we hear Rose shout from the other end of the clearing. "Thank the gods you are okay..."

As she approaches us, she pauses to catch her breath, having run all the way here.

"What happened?" she asks us. "How did you all end up in this place?"

"It was Eiden," Daren says. "He teleported us here along with another fifty mages, probably hoping to watch us kill each other, but he got bored and teleported away. Most of the others left chasing a necromancer who tried to wipe us out."

"I see..." Rose says.

She turns towards Hadrik and offers a handshake.

"You must be the shapeshifting giant from before! I'm very pleased to meet you! My name is Rose. Did you manage to find the challenger you were looking for?"

"Aye, lass," Hadrik says, grinning, as he shakes her hand. "I believe I have!"

Did he just glance towards me when he said that? I hope not.

"That is good to hear!" Rose says, smiling.

She then turns towards Azarius.

"I'm sorry, do I know you from somewhere? You look very familiar."

"I do not believe we've met before, but my portrait appears in the newspapers every once in a while. My name is Azarius. Pleased to make your acquaintance!"

choice("Continue", Ch7-Woman-mage, v_current_scene = Ch7-Woman-mage)


ID: Ch7-Invisibility
TEXT:

"Wait, are you saying that those mages are still hiding around here, invisibly?"

"No, no, of course not! What would they gain by lingering? They went invisible while covered in smoke, waited for the crowd to chase their decoys, and teleported away. I figured they learned their lesson, so I let them off with a stern warning for now."

"Barry! Kate! Daren!" we hear Rose shout from the other end of the clearing. "Thank the gods you are okay..."

As she approaches us, she pauses to catch her breath, having run all the way here.

"What happened?" she asks us. "How did you all end up in this place?"

"It was Eiden," Daren says. "He teleported us here along with another fifty mages, probably hoping to watch us kill each other, but he got bored and teleported away. Most of the others left chasing a necromancer who tried to wipe us out."

"I see..." Rose says.

She turns towards Hadrik and offers a handshake.

"You must be the shapeshifting giant from before! I'm very pleased to meet you! My name is Rose. Did you manage to find the challenger you were looking for?"

"Aye, lass," Hadrik says, grinning, as he shakes her hand. "I believe I have!"

Did he just glance towards me when he said that? I hope not.

"That is good to hear!" Rose says, smiling.

She then turns towards Azarius.

"I'm sorry, do I know you from somewhere? You look very familiar."

"I do not believe we've met before, but my portrait appears in the newspapers every once in a while. My name is Azarius. Pleased to make your acquaintance!"

choice("Continue", Ch7-Woman-mage, v_current_scene = Ch7-Woman-mage)


ID: Ch7-Woman-mage
TEXT:

"Master Azarius," says the young apprentice beside him. "I do not mean to be rude, but may I ask why you chose to intervene in this fight? Weren't you the one who taught me that banshees are foul demons that are not to be trusted?"

"Henry, plain Azarius will do," the sage says gently. "Before I answer, recall our first lesson on banshees. Why do banshees possess living hosts to survive?"

"They were originally born on the magical plane and banished to our earthen plane," Henry recites. "On this plane, they cannot endure without a physical body, so they steal living hosts. The incompatibility causes the link to decay over time, forcing them to change hosts every few decades."

"Very good!" Azarius says. "Now, do you sense any foreign, other-worldly signature radiating from this woman? Does her magical aura differ in any way from any other mortal mage you have encountered?"

"No, but... I do not understand... How can this be?..."

The sage smiles.

"That is because she isn't a banshee. She is a woman with magical powers. A woman mage, if you will."

"What?!" Daren blurts out. "How can you be so sure? You can't possibly base your entire reasoning on something so vague!"

"You surprise me, healer in armor," Azarius says thoughtfully, stroking his beard. "The way you phrased that suggests you still believe this woman to be a banshee. And yet you travel alongside her. Most peculiar."

Daren flushes at the remark, but Azarius turns his attention towards me.

"What about you, Barry? How would you define your relationship with the young woman named Kate?"

choice(""We've only just met yesterday, so I guess you could say we are acquaintances."", Ch7-Acquaintances, v_current_scene = Ch7-Acquaintances)
choice(""I see her as a friend."", Ch7-See-friend, v_current_scene = Ch7-See-friend)
choice(""She's tried to kill me twice and I keep coming back, so by some definitions that's practically married."", Ch7-Relationship, v_current_scene = Ch7-Relationship)


ID: Ch7-Acquaintances
TEXT:

"We've only just met yesterday, so I guess you could say we are acquaintances."

"Oh? But you appear to be more comfortable around her than your companion. Tell me, Barry, do you consider her to be a banshee as well?"

"What? No! Of course not!"

"Interesting... May I ask you then if you know about the Beacon of Hope?"

"Yes, she..."

I take a short glance towards Kate before I continue.

"She told me everything..."

"Wait a minute," Daren interrupts us. "Didn't Eiden say something about the Beacon this morning?"

"Yeah, he said that Kate was a survivor from there," I tell him.

"A survivor?" Daren asks. "Yeah, now I remember. And he didn't want to tell us what that meant. What is this Beacon of Hope, anyway?"

"To put it bluntly," Azarius says, "the Beacon of Hope was a human experimentation facility disguised as an orphanage. The purpose of that institution was to create mages artificially. Kate's magical powers are a direct result of those experiments."

"She's... what?..." Daren asks, too shocked to form a coherent sentence.

"Kate, you were a mage?" Rose asks, surprised.

"Yes..." Kate answers her. "What did you think I was?"

"Well, those mages we met in the forest kept shouting that you were a banshee, so I figured that's what you were... Everyone seemed to know what it meant, so I didn't want to appear dumb by asking."

Kate laughs softly.

"I guess labels aren't really very important to you, are they, Rose?" she says, smiling.

"Still, I have to admit," Hadrik says, grinning, "I didn't expect to meet two survivors from the Beacon in the same day. This tournament is really something else!"

"Two survivors?" I ask him. "You mean to say that there is another artificial mage here on this continent besides Kate?"

"Aye!" the dwarf says. "And she's mighty strong too! She took me out with almost no effort. I'm pretty sure that she was using lightning in the same way elementalists would use water or fire. Being an artificial mage sure has its perks!"

choice("Continue", Ch7-In-or-out, v_current_scene = Ch7-In-or-out)


ID: Ch7-See-friend
TEXT:

"I see her as a friend."

"A friend? Do I take it that you've known her from before this tournament?"

"No, we've only just met yesterday."

"Interesting... May I ask you then if you know about the Beacon of Hope?"

"Yes, she..."

I take a short glance towards Kate before I continue.

"She told me everything..."

"Wait a minute," Daren interrupts us. "Didn't Eiden say something about the Beacon this morning?"

"Yeah, he said that Kate was a survivor from there," I tell him.

"A survivor?" Daren asks. "Yeah, now I remember. And he didn't want to tell us what that meant. What is this Beacon of Hope, anyway?"

"To put it bluntly," Azarius says, "the Beacon of Hope was a human experimentation facility disguised as an orphanage. The purpose of that institution was to create mages artificially. Kate's magical powers are a direct result of those experiments."

"She's... what?..." Daren asks, too shocked to form a coherent sentence.

"Kate, you were a mage?" Rose asks, surprised.

"Yes..." Kate answers her. "What did you think I was?"

"Well, those mages we met in the forest kept shouting that you were a banshee, so I figured that's what you were... Everyone seemed to know what it meant, so I didn't want to appear dumb by asking."

Kate laughs softly.

"I guess labels aren't really very important to you, are they, Rose?" she says, smiling.

"Still, I have to admit," Hadrik says, grinning, "I didn't expect to meet two survivors from the Beacon in the same day. This tournament is really something else!"

"Two survivors?" I ask him. "You mean to say that there is another artificial mage here on this continent besides Kate?"

"Aye!" the dwarf says. "And she's mighty strong too! She took me out with almost no effort. I'm pretty sure that she was using lightning in the same way elementalists would use water or fire. Being an artificial mage sure has its perks!"

choice("Continue", Ch7-In-or-out, v_current_scene = Ch7-In-or-out)


ID: Ch7-Relationship
TEXT:

"She's tried to kill me twice and I keep coming back, so by some definitions that's practically married."

"Oh ho ho..." Azarius chuckles heartily. "I admire your courage, if not your survival instincts! But still, you seem to be on much friendlier terms with her than your companion. Tell me, Barry, do you consider her to be a banshee as well?"

"What? No! Of course not!"

"Interesting... May I ask you then if you know about the Beacon of Hope?"

"Yes, she..."

I take a short glance towards Kate before I continue.

"She told me everything..."

"Wait a minute," Daren interrupts us. "Didn't Eiden say something about the Beacon this morning?"

"Yeah, he said that Kate was a survivor from there," I tell him.

"A survivor?" Daren asks. "Yeah, now I remember. And he didn't want to tell us what that meant. What is this Beacon of Hope, anyway?"

"To put it bluntly," Azarius says, "the Beacon of Hope was a human experimentation facility disguised as an orphanage. The purpose of that institution was to create mages artificially. Kate's magical powers are a direct result of those experiments."

"She's... what?..." Daren asks, too shocked to form a coherent sentence.

"Kate, you were a mage?" Rose asks, surprised.

"Yes..." Kate answers her. "What did you think I was?"

"Well, those mages we met in the forest kept shouting that you were a banshee, so I figured that's what you were... Everyone seemed to know what it meant, so I didn't want to appear dumb by asking."

Kate laughs softly.

"I guess labels aren't really very important to you, are they, Rose?" she says, smiling.

"Still, I have to admit," Hadrik says, grinning, "I didn't expect to meet two survivors from the Beacon in the same day. This tournament is really something else!"

"Two survivors?" I ask him. "You mean to say that there is another artificial mage here on this continent besides Kate?"

"Aye!" the dwarf says. "And she's mighty strong too! She took me out with almost no effort. I'm pretty sure that she was using lightning in the same way elementalists would use water or fire. Being an artificial mage sure has its perks!"

choice("Continue", Ch7-In-or-out, v_current_scene = Ch7-In-or-out)


ID: Ch7-In-or-out
TEXT:

"Do you know where she was headed?" Kate asks.

"She and her friends in dark blue robes have a camp set up not far from here," Hadrik says. "From what I understood, they had no plans to leave until later tomorrow."

"Could you take me to them?!" Kate blurts out, perhaps a little too loudly.

The dwarf looks slightly taken aback by Kate's sudden request.

"Please..." she adds, this time in a lower, pleading voice.

Hadrik gives Kate a long pondering look before he decides to speak.

"Tell you what," the dwarf says. "I'll agree to lead you to your friend's camp on one condition!"

"Name it!" Kate says, her face suddenly lighting up.

Hadrik grins.

"Your friend over there has to beat me in a duel!" he says, as he turns towards me.

"Barry?!" Kate shouts, with a mixture of both shock and disappointment in her voice. "You want Barry to defeat you in a duel? You can't be serious! Why him of all people?"

"Well, I don't know if he's a stillwater or not, but I am quite positive I saw him singlehandedly fighting a dragon in the sky a few hours ago. You rarely get to meet a worthier opponent!"

Kate looks at me hesitatingly, probably wanting to see what my reaction is to all of this.

"I'm not looking for an all out fight," Hadrik says. "We wouldn't want to wear ourselves out completely before the tournament even begins. A short sparring session will do! No magic, no weapons, just like my fight with the stillwater from before. And since we're in a hurry, whoever makes the other one fall to the ground first will be declared the victor!"

The dwarf turns to me, grinning yet again.

"So, what do you say, Barry? Are you in or are you out?"

choice("Accept the challenge", Ch7-Accept, v_current_scene = Ch7-Accept)
choice("Refuse the challenge", Ch7-Refuse, v_current_scene = Ch7-Refuse)


ID: Ch7-Accept
TEXT:

#if(v_ch7_call_rose != 1 || v_ch7_watch_fight != 1) {
"Sure, why not? I'll give it a shot."

"Hah! That's the spirit!" Hadrik says.

"Barry, wait!" Kate says. "You'll get yourself killed!"

"I'll be fine!" I tell her as I move to align myself with the dwarf, leaving about twenty feet of distance between us.

Kate doesn't try to stop me anymore, but I can see that she has an uneasy look in her eyes. The others are now stepping aside in order to give us the space we need to begin our fight.

There's something that's been bothering me ever since we got teleported here. The dwarf has been maintaining a shapeshift this entire time--his aura carries the distinct resonance of altered form. Shapeshifting magic cannot fabricate a disguise as another person; it only alters scale or transforms into another species. What purpose would a dwarf disguise serve?

Then his initial appearance clicks into place. Shapeshifting magic cannot expand a mortal body tenfold into a fifty-foot colossus, but a giant can easily compress himself into a dwarf. The giant form was his true body. Hadrik is a giant, traveling small because giants were never invited to the tournament.

That explains his crushing physical power. Shrinking compresses mass, preserving nearly all original strength. Even in a friendly spar, he is not the type to dial down his blows.

His altered body proportions work against him, however. Dwarf limbs alter his center of gravity, and he admitted he is unaccustomed to running on short legs. His balance is vulnerable.

Hadrik grins, cracking his knuckles.

"Well, Barry," he says after a while. "Here I come!"

The dwarf charges straight at me without weaving. Just before reaching me, he accelerates with blinding speed, closing the gap in a blink.

Instead of throwing a punch, he extends his index finger in a heavy prod.
}
#if(v_ch7_call_rose == 1 && v_ch7_watch_fight == 1) {
"Sure, why not? I'll give it a shot."

"Hah! That's the spirit!" Hadrik says.

"Barry, wait!" Kate says. "You'll get yourself killed!"

